From 077792f2962a9ddc543f64426ce180be773c74b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Timothy J Fontaine <tjfontaine@atxconsulting.com>
Date: Tue, 12 May 2026 22:09:23 -0700
Subject: bifrost: read LOAD_PROG payload through little-endian byte-copy
 helpers

The bounded LOAD_PROG parser introduced in commit 0040 carried a
latent alignment bug: variable-length string trailers (host- and
kernel-resolved uprobe paths/basenames/symbols, USDT provider/probe
names) can leave the trailer-following `u64 file_offset`, the
`MapDef` array base, the `num_insns` u32, the `BpfInsn` array base,
and the reloc-trailer u32 fields on odd byte offsets relative to
the kmalloc-backed `ctrl_buf`. The previous parser dereferenced
those offsets through typed `*const u64` / `*const u32` /
`*const MapDef` / `*const BpfInsn` casts; any odd-length string
made the next aligned read undefined behavior on strict-alignment
guest architectures.

Replace every variable-offset typed read with explicit LE byte-copy
helpers:

  - `types::read_u32_le_unaligned(*const u8) -> u32`
  - `types::read_u64_le_unaligned(*const u8) -> u64`
  - `MapDef::read_le_unaligned(*const u8) -> MapDef`
  - `BpfInsn::read_le_unaligned(*const u8) -> BpfInsn`
  - `BpfInsn::write_le_unaligned(*mut u8, BpfInsn)`

Each helper copies the wire bytes into a stack array and recomposes
the fields through `u{32,64}::from_le_bytes`. Wire layout is
unchanged; this is purely a correctness fix for how the parser
reads the existing bytes.

The kernel-resolved uprobe (`PROBE_TYPE_UPROBE_BY_SYM`), USDT, and
host-resolved uprobe trailers are now covered by host-side odd-
trailer fixtures in `host/bifrost/src/cli/direct_load.rs` that
build LOAD_PROG payloads with adversarial string lengths and
assert the alignment of the targeted u32/u64 fields plus a
successful LE round-trip.

Also refresh the canonical sha-pin in `wire.rs` to track the
recent header refresh in `host/bifrost-wire/src/lib.rs`; the body
of the vendored copy is otherwise unchanged.

